<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Anegawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1570">1570</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Anegawa was fought in the summer of 1570 by the Ane river in northern Ōmi province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> defeated the combined armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a> and the Asakura clan. The victory did not destroy either clan at once, but it weakened them, and Nobunaga eliminated both in 1573.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-anegawa.jpg" alt="Battle of Anegawa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 立花左近 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anegawa_Historic_Battlefield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>'s moves against the castles of Odani and Yokoyama. As the Oda and Tokugawa allies marched on Odani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a> called for help from Echizen, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-yoshikage">Asakura Yoshikage</a> sent an army. The combined Azai and Asakura force came out to meet Nobunaga in the field, and he screened the Azai fort he had been reducing from Yokoyama and advanced to the southern bank of the Anegawa. The Oda and Azai later called the fight the Battle of Nomura, the Asakura the Battle of Mitamura. It was the first battle fought by the alliance of Nobunaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 30 July 1570 the Oda met the Azai on the right while the Tokugawa faced the Asakura on the left, and the fighting became a melee in the shallow river. Nobunaga gave Hashiba Hideyoshi his first command in open battle. Accounts tell that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a> charged the advancing Asakura alone, drawing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> forward to save him, and that Tokugawa troops under Tadakatsu and Sakakibara Yasumasa turned the Asakura's left flank.</p><p>Once the Asakura gave way, the Tokugawa units wheeled to strike the Azai's right flank, while the reserve of the Mino Triumvirate under Inaba Ittetsu hit their left. The Azai and Asakura lines broke. Nobunaga is often said to have used 500 arquebusiers in the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>No reliable source allows the battle to be reconstructed. The only valuable one, the Shinchōkō-ki, describes it briefly and counts 1,100 Asakura samurai killed, while much in the books of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> is fiction. A study of 1901 put the losses at about 1,700 on the Azai and Asakura side and 800 on the Oda and Tokugawa side. In October 1570 Asakura Kagetake struck back at the Battle of Shimosakamoto, killing over 750 Oda soldiers, among them Mori Yoshinari and Nobunaga's brother Oda Nobuharu. A memorial marker stands in Nomura-cho, Nagahama.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Anegawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After defeat at <b>Anegawa</b> in 1570, Nobunaga invaded in 1573, burned Ichijodani and forced Yoshikage to kill himself.</blockquote><a href="/state/asakura-clan-domain">Asakura clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeated at <b>Anegawa</b> that year, he held out until 1573, when Odani fell and he killed himself.</blockquote><a href="/state/azai-clan-domain">Azai clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
